{"name":"Anubis Black","hex":"#312231","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"anubis","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["dog-headed"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/anubis"},{"term":"black","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Absorbing all light and reflecting none; dark and hueless.","Without light."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour/color perceived in the absence of light, but also when no light is reflected, but rather absorbed.","A black dye or pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black"}],"entries":[{"title":"Anubis","blocks":[{"text":"Anubis, also known as Inpu, Inpw, Jnpw, or Anpu in Ancient Egyptian, is the god of funerary rites, protector of graves, and guide to the underworld in ancient Egyptian religion, usually depicted as a canine or a man with a canine head."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Anubis_standing.svg/330px-Anubis_standing.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anubis"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Anubis_attending_the_mummy_of_Sennedjem.jpg/500px-Anubis_attending_the_mummy_of_Sennedjem.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Anubis_attending_the_mummy_of_Sennedjem.jpg/500px-Anubis_attending_the_mummy_of_Sennedjem.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Anubis attending the mummy of the deceased.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnubis_attending_the_mummy_of_Sennedjem.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Opening_of_the_mouth_ceremony_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Opening_of_the_mouth_ceremony_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Opening_of_the_mouth_ceremony_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Opening_of_the_mouth_ceremony_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Opening_of_the_mouth_ceremony_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Opening_of_the_mouth_ceremony_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Opening of the Mouth ceremony being performed on a mummy before the tomb. Anubis attending the mummy of the deceased. Extract from the Papyrus of Hunefer, a 19th-Dynasty Book of the Dead (c. 1300 BC)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOpening_of_the_mouth_ceremony_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/BD_Hunefer_cropped_1.jpg/330px-BD_Hunefer_cropped_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/BD_Hunefer_cropped_1.jpg/330px-BD_Hunefer_cropped_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The \"weighing of the hear